<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The dollar fell 0.6% against a basket of m=
ajor currencies at one stage and is now down 0.35%. Its decline was most pr=
onounced against the Japanese yen and the Swiss franc, both seen as safe ha=
ven currencies.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Gold prices =E2=80=8Brose 2.4%, supported =
by the weaker US dollar. Spot gold climbed to $4,022 an ounce.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">US stock futures have turned positive befo=
re the Wall Street open later, as investors continue to assess the implicat=
ions of the blue wave across the US.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Dow Jones futures are up 0.4%, while S&amp=
;P 500 and Nasdaq futures have edged about 0.15% higher.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Earlier, tech-heavy Nasdaq futures fell 2.=
4%, while S&amp;P 500 futures dropped 1.4%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In bond markets, the two-year Treasury yie=
ld fell 3.5 basis points overnight and is now down 1.6bps at 4.598%, while =
the 10-year yield is 2bps lower at 4.929%. Yields move in opposite directio=
n to prices.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In Asia, Japan=E2=80=99s Nikkei lost 1.4%,=
 while Hong Kong=E2=80=99s Hang Seng rose 1.3% and South Korea=E2=80=99s Ko=
